# `widths[width]!` with width in 1 .. 9: nothing said that 9 lies inside the view, so the read gave undefined|int and count, first and `code - count` went as doubles with a test for NaN in every turn.
# Two rules refused the proof. A counter is held below the room of a view only where the view is a VALUE from in front of the loop, and `widths` is a variable of the module, read in the turn. And at the
# read itself nothing was asked in front of the typed copy, nor could the turn begin again, because step(), copied in line, has stored into the reader. A test in front runs before the first turn: what the
# turn has WRITTEN does not touch it; only other code could. So "9 < the length" is asked there, as it is of a list, and the read lies inside.
# decodeSwapped: the variable holds a shorter view at the second call: the test fails and the other copy answers NaN as plain does. decodeShrinking: a turn resizes the buffer: the turns do not leave
# views as they are, nothing is asked in front, and the reads behind the resize give undefined.
# How often the bound is asked is not pinned: since the failing way of the test leads to the loop bought once more without the question, the typed copy is walked more than once.
# f1 = decode, f2 = decodeSwapped, f3 = decodeShrinking
# OPTIONS: testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime check=Validate
# RUN: walk f1 f2 f3 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f1:Body
# CHECK: = GetTypedArrayElement {{*}}[keyIsIntegral, isInsideView]
# CHECK-LABEL: code f2:Body
# CHECK: = GetTypedArrayElement {{*}}[keyIsIntegral, isInsideView]
# CHECK-LABEL: code f3:Body
# CHECK-NOT: isInsideView
# RUN(COUNT): compile BoundsAskedOfViewsInFrontOfTypedCopies
# COUNT-NOT: statistic BoundsAskedOfViewsInFrontOfTypedCopies 0
# RUN(SHELL): run-in-shell globalThis.sink = print;
# SHELL: 34 10 NaN 9 NaN
# SHELL: exited with 0
